# The hand warmer that will not click
#
# A reusable hand warmer is a pouch of sodium acetate solution holding far
# more salt than it should be able to. You bend a little metal disc inside
# it, crystals race out from the click, and the pouch gets hot — that heat
# is the solid forming, given back.
#
# This lesson is here because the bench CANNOT do the click, and the reason
# is worth more than a demonstration would be.
register lv2

# Make the solution the way the pouch is made: a lot of salt into hot water.
add v1 water 50mL
add v1 NaOAc 40g
measure v1 thermometer
inspect v1

# Now cool it right down, past where it should have crystallised. In a real
# pouch this is the state you carry around in your coat all winter: liquid,
# cold, and loaded.
cool v1 12kJ
measure v1 thermometer

# And here is the honest part. The bench knows the solution is past its
# limit — it says how far past — and it tells you it cannot make the solid
# appear. Not "nothing happened": *why* nothing happened.
#
# The reason is not a missing number and not the wrong database. The solid
# a hand warmer makes is sodium acetate trihydrate, and no PHREEQC database
# shipped with this project defines any acetate solid at all — not
# wateq4f, not minteq.v4, not pitzer, not llnl. There is nothing for the
# aqueous engine to precipitate.
#
# Compare that with borax, one lesson over: same idea, a solution past its
# limit on cooling, and there the solid exists and comes straight out. The
# difference between the two rows is not the chemistry a child sees. It is
# whether anybody has written the phase down.
inspect v1
